{"/api/discogs/artist/103/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"103","image":"https://i.discogs.com/ZHnzJ0alLICmcuqW0fBepGG4gTsmMFakzQB13eO8a8Y/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:175/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTEwMy0w/MDEuanBn.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/JkbaofbIGVDXD_sD6xFPgAulXc7E2FNiv_NM39tw44E/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTEwMy0w/MDEuanBn.jpeg","profile":"House producer and DJ from London.\r\n","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:103","relations":{"aliases":[{"id":211370,"name":"Tom Szirtes"},{"id":234640,"name":"Seer Tesh"},{"id":470040,"name":"Lost (5)"}],"groups":null,"members":null,"namevariations":["Shu Ri Kan","Shur I Kan","Shur I Khan","Shur-I-Kan","Shur-i-ken","Shur-i-khan","Shure-I-Kann","Shuri Kan"]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/103-Shur-i-kan","type":"artist","urls":["http://ww.shur-i-kan.com","http://shur-i-kan.bandcamp.com","http://www.facebook.com/shurikanofficial","http://www.instagram.com/tomszirtes","http://www.mixcloud.com/shurikan","http://soundcloud.com/shurikan","http://twitter.com/shur_i_kan"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/1665/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"1665","image":"https://i.discogs.com/tawspe_mA2LBr0Z3D7lDn5UbsO6zQR3ml2Fy03wxl0Q/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:600/w:483/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTE2NjUt/MTY5MzcwOTUzNi0z/NjczLmpwZWc.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/E4KBVWfHlEB_4GIDseaodZIiyyIh82SfTOkCf1E1t6I/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTE2NjUt/MTY5MzcwOTUzNi0z/NjczLmpwZWc.jpeg","profile":"Though he's revered among Chicago DJs and around the world as the original master of ghetto dance music, Deeon started small, on Chicago's south side. He sold his DJ mixtapes in the parking lot where he lived, and played house parties in the projects.\r\nThese crowded living room parties started the feeling and inspiration for the sound Deeon produced, music he simply refers to as, \"for the strippers, for the street\". \r\nWhile tracks like \"Accelerate\" on Dance Mania were doing well overseas, he was getting little support or play from local Chicago clubs. Deeon calls ghetto house the \"stepchild of Chicago house\". Eventually the incredible response from his music forced the clubs to concede and play his records.\r\nThese \"techno with a ghetto edge\" tracks have found way to the decks in clubs around the world. Deeon found his home on Chicago's own Dance Mania label, where he released hits like \"3 Fine Hoes\", \"Work Dis MF\", \"Da Dick Suck\" and \"House O Matic\". \r\nPassed away in July 2023.\r\n","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:1665","relations":{"aliases":[{"id":2371,"name":"Playground Productionz"},{"id":21221,"name":"Low End Theory"},{"id":114528,"name":"Big Freek"},{"id":115961,"name":"Debo"},{"id":226496,"name":"Mute (3)"},{"id":1317878,"name":"Cozmo Dee!"},{"id":2617942,"name":"Back In Da Dayz"},{"id":4707980,"name":"Me & My 9"},{"id":7371922,"name":"Deeon Marvin Boyd"}],"groups":null,"members":null,"namevariations":["Big Fat Pimp","D. Boyd","D.Boyd","D.J. Deeon","D.J.Deeon","D.j. Deon","DJ Deeon Debo \"G\"","DJ Deon","DJ Deoon","DJ-Deeon","DJ. Deeon","DJD","Deeon","Deeon Boyd","Deoon Boyd","Dj Deeon"]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/1665-DJ-Deeon","type":"artist","urls":["https://www.facebook.com/DJDeeonFM","https://djdeeon1.bandcamp.com/","http://www.residentadvisor.net/dj/djdeeon"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/201032/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"201032","image":"https://i.discogs.com/P-IexL228J0C8eJTDh61TA_x1oQ7arEBd6Zi5jrFxmA/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:450/w:300/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTIwMTAz/Mi0xMTk0NTYxNjY0/LmpwZWc.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/O26kjY40GL7w3ZYS-1NJfr-LcnmaIdxtY456UbGgK2c/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTIwMTAz/Mi0xMTk0NTYxNjY0/LmpwZWc.jpeg","profile":"PUSHIM is a singer from Osaka Prefecture, Japan.\r\nOlder sister of singer [a3141777].","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:201032","relations":{"aliases":null,"groups":null,"members":null,"namevariations":["Pushgim"]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/201032-Pushim","type":"artist","urls":["http://www.pushim.com/"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/2664163/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"2664163","image":"https://i.discogs.com/A_rfpkqY0vBFhsJMqsa23dssuxzgQHJ2y9_H4k_rDZo/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:422/w:600/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTI2NjQx/NjMtMTU1MzA4OTE0/MS00OTM4LmpwZWc.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/Ncq8caKMJJbXYrjXmhrZj20Us-qzY8Up8oXUC4U2bsE/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTI2NjQx/NjMtMTU1MzA4OTE0/MS00OTM4LmpwZWc.jpeg","profile":"Producer and visual artist based in Glasgow, Scotland.","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:2664163","relations":{"aliases":[{"id":3026307,"name":"DJ Akeera"},{"id":7053182,"name":"Ulcer (13)"},{"id":8209997,"name":"GATR"},{"id":16502468,"name":"Alien Bong"}],"groups":null,"members":null,"namevariations":null},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/2664163-Inkke","type":"artist","urls":["https://inkke.bandcamp.com","https://www.fwdk.info","https://www.instagram.com/inkke_","http://soundcloud.com/inkke"]}},"/api/discogs/artist/3082/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"3082","image":"https://i.discogs.com/pBn8wahdKUbJt6jDefZibW80Q0CQU1lHodVRtMMmMWs/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:421/w:600/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTMwODIt/MTQ2Njk4NDg4Ny0x/MDUwLmpwZWc.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/8BEqTpDKeGldQljPScNF8qrc4M4lvfY7o_dj92t09Bg/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9BLTMwODIt/MTQ2Njk4NDg4Ny0x/MDUwLmpwZWc.jpeg","profile":"British hip-hop/downtempo production team. \r\n\r\nMark Rae and Steve Christian worked together as recording artists, producers, and remixers, since thee mid 1990s. The pair first met when they were both occupying the same rehearsal space in Ducie House, Manchester, UK. The first meeting actually took place when Steve, who is the production strength of the duo, walked past Mark’s studio and informed him, in the nicest possible way, that his music was out of key. \r\n\r\nThe duo was the first act to emerge form [l=Grand Central Records], a label that Mark Rae founded. It was a label that reflected the confident cosmopolitan of post-rave Manchester since it’s birth in 1995. Acts on the label, such as [a=Aim], [a=Only Child], [url=https://www.discogs.com/artist/3422938-Violators-Of-The-English-Language?anv=VOTEL]VOTEL[/url], and the [a=Funky Fresh Few], were all initially introduced to Rae by going into his Manchester record shop \"Fat City\". Mark Rae had acquired music makers and talented DJ’s through word of mouth, an ideal that would continue as the label expanded in size and reputation. \r\n\r\nA year after getting together, Rae & Christian enlisted the vocal skills of [a=Veba] to add a more diverse flavour to their sound. Contributions to the Grand Central compilations \"[m=158385]\" (1995) and \"[m=258232]\" (1996) attracted attention from the dance music press, and their debut album, \"[m=85435]\" (1998) fulfilled this early promise. The swagger of the singles \"[m=158399]\" (1996/1998) and \"[m=135381]\" (1999), along with Veba’s quality vocal delivery, ensured critical acclaim and admiration for the duo. \r\n\r\nTheir joint appreciation of hip-hop’s flavours did justice to the respected hip-hop acts they were able to call upon. The [a=Jungle Brothers] and [a=Jeru The Damaja] both contributed to the duo’s work, and these attributes gave the pair an edge over other late 1990s sampling contemporaries. This was coupled with a slick live show ensured that Rae & Christian had established themselves as artists in their own right. They pushed their reputation beyond underground circles also, gaining production credits with popular band [a=Texas] on albums \"[m=89704]\" (1997) and \"[m=89711]\" (1999). \r\n\r\nAt the same time as nurturing other acts on the label, their production and remix skills have been in demand by their peers ([a=Lamb], [a=The Pharcyde], [a=Wai Wan], et al) and mainstream stars such as [a=Natalie Imbruglia], [a=Simply Red], and the [a=Manic Street Preachers]. \r\n\r\nTheir second album, \"[m=144574]\" (2001), added new dimensions to their sound, as they brought in veterans such as [a=Bobby Womack], [a=Tania Maria], and [a=The Congos], which added extra spice to their music. The album marked a change in approach for [a=Mark Rae] (DJ, beat merchant, and lyricist) and Steve Christian (music maker and production-wizard), towards more live instrumentation and varied songwriting partnerships, with their sound taking on a new shape. \r\n\r\nCompleted after a period away from the industry, their third album \"[m=648379]\" (2013), has furthered their continual process to refine the combination of hip-hop ethics, traditional song writing, and 21st century production technology. \r\n","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:artist:3082","relations":{"aliases":[{"id":17727,"name":"Fat City F.C."},{"id":47355,"name":"First Priority"}],"groups":null,"members":[{"active":true,"id":16820,"name":"Mark Rae"},{"active":true,"id":48042,"name":"Steve Christian"}],"namevariations":["Christian","M Rae & S Christian","M. Rae & S. Christian","M. Rae And S. Christian","M. Rae-S. Christian","M. Rae/S. Christian","M.Rae / S.Christian","M.Rae/S. Christian","Mark Rae & Steve Christian","Mark Rae & Steven Christian","Mark Rae And Steve Christian","R&C","Rae & Chris","Rae + Christian","Rae / Christian","Rae And Christian","Rae and Christian","Rae&Christian","Rae, Christian","Rea + Christian","Vebra Veba Rae & Christian"]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/artist/3082-Rae-Christian","type":"artist","urls":["http://www.myspace.com/markraeraeandchristianarp","http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rae_%26_Christian"]}},"/api/dis